WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.379
- Welcome back, everybody.

00:00:01.379 --> 00:00:03.576
We're here with Martha
Stewart! Give it up!

00:00:03.576 --> 00:00:05.370
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.500
Our next guest is simply
one of the greatest

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.320
basketball players of all time.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.190
I'm gonna hope I get this right.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:14.160
She is a seven-time WNBA All-Star,

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.710
a three-time WNBA Champion,

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.780
including a championship this year,

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.690
and a two-time (laughs) WNBA MVP.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:24.404
So she is really good at
basketball. (chuckles)

00:00:24.404 --> 00:00:25.774
(crowd chuckles)
- [Martha] Wow.

00:00:25.774 --> 00:00:27.127
- Her new ESPN documentary is called

00:00:27.127 --> 00:00:29.400
"Candace Parker: Unapologetic."

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.100
You can see it on ESPN and ESPN+.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.846
Everybody, give it up for Candace Parker!

00:00:34.846 --> 00:00:36.373
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:00:36.373 --> 00:00:38.956
(upbeat music)

00:00:50.903 --> 00:00:52.903
- [Kelly] Come on over!

00:00:52.903 --> 00:00:55.645
(audience cheers)

00:00:55.645 --> 00:00:56.562
Oh my gosh.

00:00:58.097 --> 00:00:59.497
- The height?

00:00:59.497 --> 00:01:00.330
All right, back to back?

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:01.163
Can we back to back it?

00:01:01.163 --> 00:01:04.590
- And I'm in really big heels!

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:05.970
It's fine. It's fine.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.330
It's beautiful! You can reach
everything on the shelves.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:10.330
Have y'all ever met?

00:01:11.444 --> 00:01:13.650
- I have not!
- No, but I'm a big fan.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.298
- I am a big fan of yours!

00:01:15.298 --> 00:01:16.131
- Thank you, thank you.

00:01:16.131 --> 00:01:17.730
- I have a number of your
books, and my mom is--

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:18.750
- [Kelly] There's a hundred to pick from!

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:19.980
- Fans of both of you all.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:22.260
- So, explain the documentary.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:23.093
Why was this important for you?

00:01:23.093 --> 00:01:26.220
- I think it was so
important just at that point,

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:27.995
in this point in my life,

00:01:27.995 --> 00:01:31.050
just to show how far
I've come in my career,

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.400
but also with my family.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.640
So coming off a successful rookie season,

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.090
I had my daughter my second year.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.440
And that was the first question,

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.320
was "What about your career?"

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.910
And then when I'd be on the road,

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.327
people would always ask me,

00:01:46.327 --> 00:01:47.910
"Who's taking care of your daughter?"

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.617
- Oh my God. Do you ask men that?

00:01:49.617 --> 00:01:50.640
- And that would be the thing.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.100
I'm like, "Do you ask
LeBron where his kids are?"

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.380
- No, you don't, because
you call that babysitting.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.380
And I'm done.
- Exactly. Exactly.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:58.530
- Yeah.
- That's how it is.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:01:59.363
- It drives me nuts.
- So I just noticed

00:01:59.363 --> 00:02:00.537
the difference.
- Yeah.

00:02:00.537 --> 00:02:02.340
- And you can follow your dreams

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:03.930
and still bring your family along.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.038
- Absolutely.

00:02:05.038 --> 00:02:05.940
(audience applauds)

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:10.230
You know, I find it more powerful,

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.420
I find it more powerful that
you had all that weight on you,

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:13.253
everybody was like,

00:02:13.253 --> 00:02:14.790
"Oh my God, she's going to be the face,"

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.350
and then you did do something

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.960
that was very much a feminine thing to do.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:20.594
We have babies. Ha-ha.

00:02:20.594 --> 00:02:23.010
And it's a beautiful thing.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.290
What sucks for you, and
I just want to apologize

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.410
for humans that are mean,

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.480
is that that should have been
a beautiful time for you.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:31.920
That should have been a
beautiful moment for you,

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:32.760
to be pregnant,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.770
'cause you always wanted
to have a kid, right?

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.719
- Always.
- Yeah.

00:02:36.719 --> 00:02:38.490
- From a young age, I was playing house.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:41.340
I had four kids, all my names picked out

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.300
for my kids before--

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:43.734
- We were so different.

00:02:43.734 --> 00:02:45.234
(everyone laughs)

00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:46.578
- I wanted to--
- What are the names?

00:02:46.578 --> 00:02:48.870
- Well, my daughter's name is Lailaa

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.313
and my son's name is Airr.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:52.980
But at the time, you know, those change,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.260
'cause you get a partner and
they have an opinion too,

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.312
and you have to kinda
compromise and all that.

00:02:57.312 --> 00:02:58.147
- And you're like, "Oh my
God, I guess yours is better."

00:02:58.147 --> 00:03:00.410
- "Yeah, and I guess you
get to name them, too."

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.300
But I always wanted to be
a mom, and you're right,

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.313
and I think this time around,

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.700
when my wife and I were expecting,

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:11.310
it was like I wanted to be able

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.920
to post pictures of her pregnant.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.642
I wanted to be able to share

00:03:15.642 --> 00:03:16.500
the joy.
- I'm glad

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:17.333
you celebrated that though.

00:03:17.333 --> 00:03:19.267
It empowers other people to be able

00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:21.480
to balance both if they'd like.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.010
- And to be able to balance and not worry

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.410
about what the perception is,

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.260
and not doing it because of others.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.160
You do it for yourself.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.380
And so I think that's the
biggest thing with motherhood,

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.270
is it's taught me to continue to strive

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.990
to what makes me happy and
what makes my family happy.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:37.823
- Yeah!

00:03:37.823 --> 00:03:39.067
And it's so interesting,
too, when they say,

00:03:39.067 --> 00:03:39.960
"Oh, who's watching your child?"

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.630
I'm like, "Who is watching
any person's child

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:43.890
that has a job?"

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:44.723
You know what I'm saying?

00:03:44.723 --> 00:03:46.268
Grandma, a nanny, somebody.
- That's true. That's true.

00:03:46.268 --> 00:03:47.889
(audience applauds)

00:03:47.889 --> 00:03:49.994
- Well, we have all dealt with divorce,

00:03:49.994 --> 00:03:52.980
which is horrific.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.710
What's it like for
y'all in the public eye?

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:57.345
Mine was very public.

00:03:57.345 --> 00:03:59.280
That's very hard.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.660
'Cause I know a lot of
people deal with divorce,

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:02.130
even at work, and it's
like, you have to show up,

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:03.450
you have to smile, you
have to do your job,

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.340
but you're kinda dying inside.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.753
How is that for y'all?

00:04:08.073 --> 00:04:09.930
- Eh. (chuckles)
(audience laughs)

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.065
- No one wants this therapy session

00:04:12.065 --> 00:04:12.898
right now.
- I was just like,

00:04:12.898 --> 00:04:13.815
yeah, yeah.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:18.086
- I got divorced during
my wedding book tour.

00:04:18.086 --> 00:04:18.919
- Oh my God!

00:04:18.919 --> 00:04:20.490
- And I had to talk about weddings

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:22.770
and wedding dresses and
how to have a wedding.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:23.603
- What?

00:04:23.603 --> 00:04:24.436
- And that's when I had

00:04:24.436 --> 00:04:25.720
to get divorced.
- Oh my

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:26.553
goodness.
- It was horrible.

00:04:26.553 --> 00:04:28.190
- You just trumped both of us!
- Right?

00:04:29.144 --> 00:04:30.060
You win.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:32.460
- Oh my God! That must have been so hard!

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:33.930
- And it took a long time. Dragged out.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:35.070
Don't drag it out.

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:36.120
That's my advice.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:36.953
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:36.953 --> 00:04:38.520
- Don't drag it out.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.560
- Wow.
- Well, you try not to, yeah.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:41.520
- That's a hard follow.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.677
- Yeah, I feel like my life,

00:04:43.677 --> 00:04:46.830
I'm either killing it
in the business world,

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:49.770
like with my purpose and my job, my love,

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:51.300
or I'm killing it in romance.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.710
And it's very hard to have
both for me, apparently.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:59.460
But how do you feel?

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:00.870
Did you feel like it was a struggle?

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:02.100
'Cause your life has changed.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.840
You were with a man,
now you're with a woman.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.160
You got to tell this whole story.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.779
Your life has changed dramatically.

00:05:06.779 --> 00:05:09.000
That's hard to do in general,

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.027
but also in the public eye,
that's a lotta eyes watching.

00:05:12.027 --> 00:05:14.130
- And I think sometimes, you know,

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.330
in the documentary I talk about

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:18.120
how it took a long time for me to be okay

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.550
with my journey and myself,

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:21.720
and the understanding

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.450
that not everybody is going to love it,

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:26.580
but I just ask that you respect it.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.324
And so, with that being said--

00:05:28.324 --> 00:05:30.228
(audience applauds)

00:05:30.228 --> 00:05:31.500
- [Kelly] Yeah!

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.620
- And I think you're a better mom

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:38.580
because you're pursuing
your goals and your dreams,

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:43.580
and I know I'm a better mom
because I'm pursuing my dreams.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:44.933
- I tell my daughter that.

00:05:44.933 --> 00:05:45.766
- Yes!
- My daughter will be like,

00:05:45.766 --> 00:05:46.980
"Why can't you come to this?

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:47.910
Somebody else's mom comes to this."

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:49.620
I'm like, "Okay, well, I have a job,

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:50.820
and I just want you to look at me

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:52.410
and know that when you
grow up and you have a job,

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:53.490
you can also have kids, too,

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:55.090
and you can also make it all happen."

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:56.430
- And you can balance,
and you wanna be there,

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:58.470
and there are things
that you have to miss.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:05:59.430
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:00.263
- But--
- And there are things

00:06:00.263 --> 00:06:01.096
my mother missed.

00:06:01.096 --> 00:06:03.003
I'm fine.
- You're okay.

00:06:03.003 --> 00:06:03.854
- You grew up okay.
- I'm like,

00:06:03.854 --> 00:06:04.740
"Everybody's gonna make everything?"

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:05.573
No, you don't.

00:06:05.573 --> 00:06:07.680
People miss games,
people miss performances.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:08.970
That's okay.
- A hundred percent.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.980
- You try your best, but that's okay.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.260
I hate when people are like,
"Oh, you missed that one?"

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.240
I'm like, "I made the other 10!"

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:16.230
I was like, you know?

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.981
- Listen, I am not the cupcake mom.

00:06:17.981 --> 00:06:18.870
You know, the one

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.000
that does all the cupcakes?
- I don't bake.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.650
- Have you seen the viral thing now

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:27.000
with the lunches where they
cut dinosaurs into eggs

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:27.833
and all that stuff?
- Yeah.

00:06:27.833 --> 00:06:30.540
- No, my daughter got lucky
if the sandwich was together.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:31.620
You know what I mean?

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:33.420
- Yeah.
- But she's good. You know?

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.403
- [Kelly] Everybody's different.

00:06:35.403 --> 00:06:36.236
- It's the thought that counts, right?

00:06:36.236 --> 00:06:37.110
- Yeah, you were, yeah.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:39.254
I was like, she probably did
do cookie cutter dinosaurs.

00:06:39.254 --> 00:06:40.587
- Yeah, she did dinosaur.

00:06:40.587 --> 00:06:42.410
You did the shapes cut out. (laughs)

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:43.321
(audience applauds)

00:06:43.321 --> 00:06:45.060
- She's, like, "Well, I kinda kill

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:48.033
with that too."
- She goes "I'm outta this."

00:06:48.033 --> 00:06:48.866
- Oh my God.

00:06:48.866 --> 00:06:49.830
Did you ever feel that you had to pick

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:51.520
between love and work?

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:52.353
- I didn't buy the pony.

00:06:52.353 --> 00:06:53.787
- [Kelly] I didn't buy the pony! (laughs)

00:06:53.787 --> 00:06:55.775
- And that keeps haunting me.

00:06:55.775 --> 00:06:57.205
- [Kelly] Oh my God.

00:06:57.205 --> 00:06:58.567
- Get the pony? Okay. (laughs)
- Oh my god. Oh my god.

00:06:58.567 --> 00:07:00.617
- I hope my daughter's not watching this.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:02.700
Christmas is coming up.

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.176
Oh my gosh.
- She's like,

00:07:04.176 --> 00:07:05.183
"Martha said that you
were gonna get me a pony."

00:07:05.183 --> 00:07:06.016
(audience applauds)

00:07:06.016 --> 00:07:07.230
Martha made history

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:10.050
with her Sports Illustrated
Swimsuit issue cover

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:11.933
at 81 years old.

00:07:11.933 --> 00:07:14.766
(audience cheers)

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.513
- First of all, ma'am.

00:07:19.561 --> 00:07:20.394
- [Candace] Goodness.

00:07:20.394 --> 00:07:21.360
- Every 81-year-old out there

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.847
is like, "This is not like the others."

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:28.680
Why now? This is incredible.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:29.703
- I wonder.

00:07:30.576 --> 00:07:32.520
They never asked me before.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.050
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:35.760
- I would've done it when I was 40.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:37.587
I looked great at 40.

00:07:37.587 --> 00:07:39.936
(audience applauds)
(Kelly laughs)

00:07:39.936 --> 00:07:41.853
- Well, they think you
look great at 81 too.

00:07:41.853 --> 00:07:44.640
So it's just, I love
that you you did it too.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.600
I think it was the most
popular one, right?

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:48.450
Of the covers?
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.543
We got like many, many
billions of impressions.

00:07:52.415 --> 00:07:54.750
- It was crazy.
(audience applauds)

00:07:54.750 --> 00:07:57.770
I do think though, you age really well.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:00.720
So I don't know if that's
comparable to the rest of us.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.720
But I don't know, did
your mother influence you?

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:05.370
Didn't she kind of influence your attitude

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:06.860
about aging?
- Oh, mom was great.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:10.080
Mom was beautiful and with it

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:13.200
and totally alert and intelligent.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.090
And she raised six kids.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:16.230
- No.

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:20.203
- And she was great until the
day she passed away at 93.

00:08:20.203 --> 00:08:21.036
- [Kelly] 93?

00:08:21.036 --> 00:08:22.470
- And she never sick.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:25.950
And she was fantastic. She
was swimming every day.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:27.540
You know, she's an amazing woman.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:28.840
- What a great role model as well.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.450
- Very, very good role model.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:32.820
- Well you are following in
those footsteps, aren't ya?

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:33.840
I was like wow!

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:36.570
So I don't know, do
you feel like with age,

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:39.300
like people get put in boxes,
whether it's in the industry,

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:40.920
I'm sure they do as well in sports.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.033
Like do you feel that?

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:44.940
- I feel like to some extent,

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:48.270
the world likes putting
women, especially in boxes.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:50.370
And then getting mad when
they don't fit in the box

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:51.660
that they put 'em in.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:54.300
And I think the limitations
that they put on you

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.460
and what you're capable
of, I'm six foot four,

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:57.750
and when I was younger,

00:08:57.750 --> 00:08:58.620
I didn't wanna play.
- You're short.

00:08:58.620 --> 00:08:59.550
- I'm short.
- I know.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:00.540
- That's what I thought.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:01.740
That's why I wanted to play point guard.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:03.600
And everybody was like, no,
you have to stand by the basket

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:07.830
and you know, shoot hooks and do all that.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:10.440
And for me it was like, I
really loved Alan Iverson.

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:11.940
He was my favorite player growing up.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:13.772
- Yeah.
- Right?

00:09:13.772 --> 00:09:18.090
And my dad was like, "Go
dribble. Like go pass."

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:21.390
And I think now we're seeing
the value of being versatile

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:22.870
and playing different positions.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:25.800
But yeah, of course the world
puts limitations on you.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:27.270
And I think it's just your job

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:30.057
to kind of be like whatever, right?

00:09:30.057 --> 00:09:31.350
- No. Yeah, say no thank you.

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:32.183
- [Candace] You're like nah.

00:09:32.183 --> 00:09:34.509
- Nah. Just an N-A-H.

00:09:34.509 --> 00:09:37.830
So why have you chosen against retiring?

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:39.840
You said you were gonna retire, right?

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:40.680
- Well, first--

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:41.513
- [Kelly] Or somebody else said.

00:09:41.513 --> 00:09:43.230
- I've been pondering
retirement from basketball

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:46.500
at like 34, 35, 36, 37.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:47.333
- How old are you?

00:09:47.333 --> 00:09:48.210
- I'm 37 right now.

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:49.740
- Oh my gosh.
- Still pondering.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:50.640
Pondering retirement.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:54.720
But what's crazy is now
my teammates are closer

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:58.110
to my daughter's age than my age.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:09:59.880
So they're over there doing TikToks,

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:01.500
and I'm like, what are y'all doing?

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:03.696
Like how do you know all of these dances

00:10:03.696 --> 00:10:04.950
and these dance moves?

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:06.360
And I'm like, grandma on the team.

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.220
That's like, wait, what's
the lyric to that song?

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:09.180
You're singing what?

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:10.410
Like, that's me in the locker room.

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:11.846
So, I'm--

00:10:11.846 --> 00:10:12.840
- And you're only 37.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:14.010
- But it's coming.

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:16.967
'Cause when I started
realizing we were closer--

00:10:16.967 --> 00:10:18.870
- Tic Tac don't, or tic-tac-toe,
whatever it's called.

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:20.974
- See? See, exactly.
- TikTok don't matter,

00:10:20.974 --> 00:10:22.318
it's how you play on the,

00:10:22.318 --> 00:10:24.237
yeah, it's how you play on the court.

00:10:24.237 --> 00:10:25.327
That's what matters.
- That's true.

00:10:25.327 --> 00:10:26.610
But when you start
seeing that you daughter

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.490
is having more in common with
your teammates than you are,

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:30.840
you're like, "Wow,

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:32.940
this is real life."
- Is your daughter gonna play?

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:34.710
- She plays volleyball and basketball.

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:35.543
- [Kelly] Oh yeah.

00:10:35.543 --> 00:10:37.620
- And she loves, yeah,
she loves basketball.

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:40.110
- I didn't know this,
but some WNBA players,

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:43.710
they have to get second
jobs because the pay.

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:45.630
Is that still like happening?

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:46.950
- A hundred percent.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:48.270
For the first 10 years of my career,

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:51.210
I went from April to October in the WNBA

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:53.190
and from October to April overseas.

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:56.220
So I played in Russia, I played in Turkey,

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.260
I played two years in China.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:00.960
And my daughter was a five month old baby,

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:04.140
packed her up, 12 bags, went to Russia.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:06.510
That was what, and still is, the reality

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:08.250
of a lot of WNBA players.

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:10.950
And you know, I think it's
just important to understand

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:15.450
that like when that, you know,
corporations, organizations,

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:18.720
they put women on
television, women's sports,

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:20.550
I mean now everybody's catching up.

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:21.570
But it's been a thing.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.880
It's just that the
visibility wasn't there.

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:25.620
And so now I think the more visibility

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:29.500
that women's sports has,
the more sponsorship,

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:30.750
equal pay.
- More equality, right.

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:31.680
- Exactly.
- Yeah.

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:32.910
- It's gonna happen.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:34.310
- Yeah 'cause that's insane,

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:37.440
the difference between men and women.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.677
We need a commercial break, everybody.

00:11:39.677 --> 00:11:40.510
(audience applauds)

